    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d626">

                <pb facs="0756" n="682" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav01/pg682"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/2980</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Moscow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Poole</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-09-04">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 4, 1918</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received October 16.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that under date
                    of September 4, I sent a personal note to the Commissar for Foreign Affairs
                    through the Minister of the Netherlands, who was in Moscow at the time
                    negotiating on behalf of the neutrals respecting the several questions <pb
                        facs="0757" n="683" xml:id="pg683"/> which had arisen between the Entente
                    powers and the Bolshevik government. A copy is enclosed.</p>
                <p>I also enclose copies of the telegrams which accompanied the note to the
                        Commissar.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d626-f1"/> The Department will observe that
                    in No. 2 the last three sentences of the text as sent to the Department were
                    suppressed. The reason is obvious. In the copy of this telegram furnished to the
                    Commissar the next to the last sentence of the third [fourth] paragraph was also
                    altered, reading: “An irresponsible government is venting its desperation in the
                    daily massacre of untold innocents.”</p>
                <p>This step was taken with the approval of my Allied colleagues and the Minister of
                    the Netherlands. I felt that it was a desperate situation justifying any measure
                    which might conceivably restrain the Bolsheviki in their lawless and inhuman
                    career.</p>
                <p>In connection with telegram No. 1, I invite the Department’s attention to the
                    enclosed excerpt from the report of the Commissar for Foreign Affairs before the
                    meeting of the All-Russian Central Executive Committee on September 2.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d626-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">DeWitt C. Poole</hi>, Jr.
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d626-a1.xml" xpointer="d626-a1"/>
            </div>
